Article 4

The scope of regulation of protection for women and children includes: a. Rights of women and children;
b. Duties and authoritiesc. Handling of acts of violence;
e. Financing;
f. Supervision;
g. Community participation.

Article 5

In the implementation of child protection in the region, children have the right:
a. to live, grow and develop and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity and worth and receive guidance and protection from violence, negligence and discrimination;
b. to a valid birth certificate obtained and/or sought by their parents in accordance with the provisions of applicable laws and regulations; c. to worship according to their religion, think and express themselves in accordance with their level of intelligence and age under parental guidance;
d. to know, be raised and cared for by their own parents; e. in the event that for some reason parents cannot guarantee the growth and development of the child or the child is in a neglected state, then the child has the right to be cared for or adopted as a foster child or adopted child by another person in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations; f. to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual and social needs; g. to obtain education and teaching according to their abilities, interests and talents: h. to express opinions and have their opinions heard; a. to rest and utilize time to develop talents and abilities: j. to receive protection from family, community and Regional Government against discriminatory treatment and exploitation both economically and socially, neglect, cruelty, violence, abuse, injustice and other mistreatment;
k. to regain formal or non-formal education for children who drop out of school;l. to receive exclusive breast milk (ASI) as a priority;

m. to receive guidance
n. to obtain other rights in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 6

In the implementation of protection of women in the region, women have the right to: a. basic needs;
o. comfort;
d. security: maximum health services in facing acts of violence;
e. assistance services, legal aid and spiritual guidance in facing acts of violence; andg. confidentiality regarding the problems faced. a7

Article 13

1. The regional government carries out prevention of the transplantation of children’s body organs to other parties and acts of violence against children.
2. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out in the form of activities which include:
a. Detection and early intervention
b. Socialization and education;
c. Seminars or workshops;
d. Preparation of an effective information system;
e. Cooperation with institutions that have activities in preventing acts of violence against children.

Article 16

1. The regional government carries out prevention of acts of violence against women.
2. Prevention efforts against acts of violence against women are carried out in the form of activities which include:
a. Regional Action Plan
b. Socialization;
c. Seminars or workshops;
d. Preparation of an effective information system;
e. Cooperation with institutions that have activities in preventing acts of violence against women.

Article 19

1. Related parties with whom the Regent cooperates as referred to in Article 14 paragraph (1) include, among others:
a. Social community organizations, institutions, or orphanages that have activities related to the protection, care, accommodation, guidance, and empowerment of children and/or women;
b. Social workers or volunteers;
c. Advocates;
d. Clergy
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in the form of a cooperation agreement.

Article 29

1. Forms of community participation may include: a. Supervision; or b. Provision of assistance;

(2) Community supervision includes activities:

a. observing or seeing the condition of the fulfillment of the rights of women and children; and b. providing information on any condition that tends to lead to acts of violence against women and children to the police and/or regional government.

3. Provision of assistance as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b shall be in the form of: a. securing or temporary shelter for victims;

. b. financial support to meet the needs of victims according to the community's capacity;

c. Financial assistance and/ or facilities to the regional government in supporting the implementation of protection for women and children.
